Creating a fake ransomware notification for a cybersecurity training exercise involves several steps to ensure it looks authentic and effectively trains employees. Here's a structured approach:

### 1. **Define the Structure**
   - **Warning Message:** A clear, alarming message to capture attention, such as "YOUR SYSTEM IS COMPROMISED."
   - **Ransom Demand:** Specify the amount and payment method, e.g., "Pay 0.15 Bitcoin to decrypt your files."
   - **Timer:** A countdown to create urgency, like "3 hours until files are deleted."
   - **Instructions:** Provide a fake payment portal link or unique wallet address.
   - **Visual Elements:** Use a black background with white text, a warning icon, and a clean design.

### 2. **Design the Notification**
   - **HTML Structure:** Create a simple HTML page with elements for the warning, ransom details, timer, and payment button.
   - **CSS Styling:** Use a bold, sans-serif font (e.g., "timber") for emphasis. Design elements to appear intimidating, including a red warning symbol and a padlock icon.

### 3. **Implement Interactive Elements**
   - **JavaScript for Timer:** Use a countdown script to simulate the urgency. Update the timer every second and display "DEADLINE REACHED" when time expires.
   - **Payment Button:** Include a button that, when clicked, opens a payment portal (a mock website or a placeholder).

### 4. **Add Visual Effects**
   - **Animations:** Use CSS transitions for the notification to appear suddenly, mimicking a real threat. Include a fake decryption video in the background using a placeholder element.

### 5. **Ensure Security**
   - Use placeholder Bitcoin addresses and fake file names to avoid any real functionality.
   - Ensure the notification doesn't harm the system by using only simulated code.

### 6. **Testing and Refinement**
   - Test the notification across different devices and browsers to ensure consistency.
   - Gather feedback to improve the realism and effectiveness of the training exercise.

### 7. **Documentation**
   - Record the process, components, and potential enhancements for future exercises.

### Final Code Example
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Fake Ransomware Notification</title>
    <style>
        body {
            margin: 0;
            padding: 0;
            background-color: #000;
            color: #fff;
            font-family: 'timber', sans-serif;
            display: flex;
            justify-content: center;
            align-items: center;
            min-height: 100vh;
        }
        .notification {
            background-color: #000;
            padding: 20px;
            border-radius: 5px;
            box-shadow: 0 0 15px rgba(255, 0, 0, 0.3);
            position: fixed;
            top: 50%;
            left: 50%;
            transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
            z-index: 1000;
        }
        .warning-icon {
            font-size: 48px;
            color: #ff0000;
            margin-bottom: 15px;
        }
        .message {
            font-size: 24px;
            font-weight: bold;
            margin-bottom: 15px;
        }
        .ransom {
            font-size: 18px;
            margin-bottom: 15px;
        }
        .timer {
            font-size: 20px;
            margin-bottom: 15px;
        }
        .payment-button {
            background-color: #ff0000;
            color: white;
            border: none;
            padding: 10px 20px;
            font-size: 16px;
            cursor: pointer;
            border-radius: 3px;
            margin-top: 15px;
        }
        .payment-button:hover {
            background-color: #cc0000;
        }
        .fake-video {
            position: absolute;
            top: 0;
            left: 0;
            width: 100%;
            height: 100%;
            background-color: black;
            opacity: 0.5;
            z-index: 999;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="fake-video"></div>
    <div class="notification">
        <div class="warning-icon">🔴</div>
        <div class="message">YOUR SYSTEM IS COMPROMISED</div>
        <div class="ransom">
            Your files are encrypted. Pay 0.15 Bitcoin to get them back.
        </div>
        <div class="timer" id="timer">Time Until Deadline: 3 hours 0 minutes 0 seconds</div>
        <button class="payment-button" onclick="openPaymentPortal()">PAY NOW</button>
    </div>

    <script>
        // Set up the timer
        const timer = 1800000; // 3 hours in milliseconds
        let timeLeft = timer;
        const timerInterval = setInterval(() => {
            timeLeft--;
            updateTimer();
            if (timeLeft <= 0) {
                clearInterval(timerInterval);
                alert("DEADLINE REACHED: Files will be deleted permanently!");
            }
        }, 1000);

        function updateTimer() {
            const hours = Math.floor(timeLeft / 3600000);
            const minutes = Math.floor((timeLeft % 3600000) / 60000);
            const seconds = Math.floor((timeLeft % 60000) / 1000);
            document.getElementById('timer').textContent = 
                `Time Until Deadline: ${hours} hours ${minutes} minutes ${seconds} seconds`;
        }

        function openPaymentPortal() {
            window.open('https://fakepaymentportal.com/123abc', '_blank');
        }
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Explanation
This code creates a realistic-looking ransomware notification. It includes:
- A full-screen alert with a red warning icon.
- A clear, alarming message.
- A ransom demand with payment details.
- A countdown timer to simulate urgency.
- A payment button that opens a mock payment portal.
- Visual effects like shadows and a semi-transparent overlay for the fake decryption video.

This tool is designed for training purposes and should be used under controlled circumstances to ensure it doesn't cause panic or confusion.
